 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# e5 · Linear vs non-linear: Elastic Net vs XGBoost\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sub-question:** *Does non-linearity add anything?* Compare Elastic Net (EN) and XGBoost (XGB) forecasts of monthly log realised volatility, **holding the information set fixed**, in three cells:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Cell | Information set | EN model | XGB model |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 1 | Full macro + VIX | `en_full_macro` | `xgb_full_macro` |\n",
    "| 2 | VIX only | `en_vix_only` | `xgb_vix_only` |\n",
    "| 3 | Macro without VIX | `en_no_vix` | `xgb_no_vix` |\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### What this notebook does\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Loss functions.** Forecasts are scored with **MSE on log RV** and **QLIKE on RV levels**.\n",
    "2. **Significance test: Diebold–Mariano (DM).** The professor's guidance is that DM is fine for **non-nested** pairs and that *\"EN vs XGB on the same inputs\"* is non-nested. Clark–West (CW) is for **nested** pairs only, such as EN no-macro vs EN full-macro or GARCH vs GARCH-MIDAS. It is not used here.\n",
    "\n",
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Setup and configuration\n",
    "\n",
    "All choices live in one place. Nothing below the config cell is hard-coded."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from scipy import stats\n",
    "\n",
    "pd.set_option(\"display.width\", 160)\n",
    "pd.set_option(\"display.float_format\", lambda x: f\"{x:,.4f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---------- Paths (relative to models/forecasting/evaluation/) ----------\n",
    "FORECASTS_DIR = Path(\"../forecasts\")      # where the tidy CSVs live\n",
    "RESULTS_DIR   = Path(\"../results\")        # where output CSVs go\n",
    "EN_FILE       = \"en_forecasts.csv\"\n",
    "XGB_FILE      = \"xgb_forecasts_expanding_tidy.csv\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---------- Comparison cells: key -> label ----------\n",
    "# Model names are built as f\"en_{key}\" and f\"xgb_{key}\".\n",
    "INFO_SETS = {\n",
    "    \"full_macro\": \"Full macro + VIX\",\n",
    "    \"vix_only\":   \"VIX only\",\n",
    "    \"no_vix\":     \"Macro without VIX\",\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---------- Forecast horizon ----------\n",
    "H = 1  \n",
    "\n",
    "# ---------- DM test settings ----------\n",
    "HLN_CORRECTION = True    \n",
    "ALPHA          = 0.05\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---------- QLIKE definition ----------\n",
    "QLIKE_NORMALISED = True \n",
    "\n",
    "# ---------- Sub-samples (\"buckets\") ----------\n",
    "# ❓ CONFIRM: your layout mentions \"per ETF + bucket\". I do not know how your buckets are defined,\n",
    "# so by default only the full sample is used. Add entries as (start, end) date strings, e.g.\n",
    "#   \"My bucket\": (\"2008-01-01\", \"2009-12-31\")\n",
    "SUBSAMPLES = {\n",
    "    \"Full sample\": (None, None),\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---------- Extras ----------\n",
    "POOL_ETFS    = True  \n",
    "SAVE_RESULTS = True  "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Load the two forecast files and run integrity checks\n",
    "\n",
    "Each file is a tidy long table with one row per `model × ETF × forecast-origin date`. The columns I use are:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Column | Meaning |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| `actual_logRV`, `actual_RV` | Realised outcome for the month being forecast (log and level) |\n",
    "| `fc_log` | Forecast of log RV, used for the **MSE** |\n",
    "| `fc_level` | Forecast of RV in levels, used for **QLIKE** |\n",
    "\n",
    "`en_forecasts.csv` also has `target_date`. The XGB file has only `date`. Both files are joined on `(etf, date)`, and the check below confirms the realised values then agree exactly, which would not be true if the date convention differed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def _read(path):\n",
    "    if not path.exists():\n",
    "        raise FileNotFoundError(\n",
    "            f\"{path.resolve()} not found. Edit FORECASTS_DIR / file names in the config cell.\"\n",
    "        )\n",
    "    return pd.read_csv(path, parse_dates=[\"date\"])\n",
    "\n",
    "en_raw  = _read(FORECASTS_DIR / EN_FILE)\n",
    "xgb_raw = _read(FORECASTS_DIR / XGB_FILE)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"EN  rows:\", len(en_raw),  \"| models:\", sorted(en_raw.model.unique()))\n",
    "print(\"XGB rows:\", len(xgb_raw), \"| models:\", sorted(xgb_raw.model.unique()))\n",
    "print(\"ETFs  EN :\", sorted(en_raw.etf.unique()))\n",
    "print(\"ETFs  XGB:\", sorted(xgb_raw.etf.unique()))\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- integrity checks (fail loudly rather than silently produce wrong numbers) ----\n",
    "need = [\"model\", \"etf\", \"date\", \"actual_logRV\", \"actual_RV\", \"fc_log\", \"fc_level\"]\n",
    "for name, df in [(\"EN\", en_raw), (\"XGB\", xgb_raw)]:\n",
    "    assert not set(need) - set(df.columns), f\"{name}: missing columns {set(need) - set(df.columns)}\"\n",
    "    assert df[need].notna().all().all(), f\"{name}: NaNs found in required columns\"\n",
    "    assert not df.duplicated([\"model\", \"etf\", \"date\"]).any(), f\"{name}: duplicate (model, etf, date) rows\"\n",
    "    assert (df.actual_RV > 0).all() and (df.fc_level > 0).all(), f\"{name}: non-positive RV or forecast level\"\n",
    "print(\"\\nIntegrity checks passed (no NaNs, no duplicate keys, all RV and forecast levels > 0).\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Which models are used / ignored\n",
    "used_en  = [f\"en_{k}\"  for k in INFO_SETS]\n",
    "used_xgb = [f\"xgb_{k}\" for k in INFO_SETS]\n",
    "missing = [m for m in used_en if m not in set(en_raw.model)] + [m for m in used_xgb if m not in set(xgb_raw.model)]\n",
    "assert not missing, f\"Models not found in files: {missing}\"\n",
    "ignored = sorted(set(en_raw.model) - set(used_en))\n",
    "print(\"EN models ignored in this notebook:\", ignored)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Put EN and XGB on the same sample\n",
    "\n",
    "A forecast comparison is only valid if both models are scored on **exactly the same ETF-months**. For each information set, I inner-join the EN and XGB rows on `(etf, date)`, so any month that only one model forecasts is dropped. The cell reports how many rows that removes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "pieces, drop_log = [], []\n",
    "for key, label in INFO_SETS.items():\n",
    "    en  = en_raw.loc[en_raw.model == f\"en_{key}\",  [\"etf\", \"date\", \"actual_logRV\", \"actual_RV\", \"fc_log\", \"fc_level\"]]\n",
    "    xgb = xgb_raw.loc[xgb_raw.model == f\"xgb_{key}\", [\"etf\", \"date\", \"actual_logRV\", \"actual_RV\", \"fc_log\", \"fc_level\"]]\n",
    "\n",
    "    m = en.merge(xgb, on=[\"etf\", \"date\"], how=\"inner\", suffixes=(\"_en\", \"_xgb\"))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Both files must agree on what was realised\n",
    "    assert np.allclose(m.actual_logRV_en, m.actual_logRV_xgb) and np.allclose(m.actual_RV_en, m.actual_RV_xgb), \\\n",
    "        f\"{key}: realised values differ between EN and XGB files, so the date alignment is wrong\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    drop_log.append(dict(info_set=label, EN_rows=len(en), XGB_rows=len(xgb), common_rows=len(m),\n",
    "                         EN_only=len(en) - len(m), XGB_only=len(xgb) - len(m),\n",
    "                         first_common=m.date.min().date(), last_common=m.date.max().date()))\n",
    "    m = m.rename(columns={\"actual_logRV_en\": \"actual_logRV\", \"actual_RV_en\": \"actual_RV\"}) \\\n",
    "         .drop(columns=[\"actual_logRV_xgb\", \"actual_RV_xgb\"])\n",
    "    m.insert(0, \"info_set\", key)\n",
    "    pieces.append(m)\n",
    "\n",
    "panel = pd.concat(pieces, ignore_index=True)\n",
    "display(pd.DataFrame(drop_log))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Same ETF-months across all cells? (so cells are comparable with each other)\n",
    "sizes = panel.groupby(\"info_set\").size()\n",
    "print(\"\\nRows per cell:\", sizes.to_dict())\n",
    "print(\"Observations per ETF per cell:\\n\", panel.groupby([\"info_set\", \"etf\"]).size().unstack())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Loss functions\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $y_t=\\log RV_t$ and $\\hat y_t$ the forecast of log RV (`fc_log`). Let $RV_t$ be the realised variance and $F_t$ the level forecast (`fc_level`).\n",
    "\n",
    "**MSE on log RV**\n",
    "\n",
    "$$L^{MSE}_t=(y_t-\\hat y_t)^2$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**QLIKE on RV levels** (normalised form, if `QLIKE_NORMALISED=True`)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$L^{QLIKE}_t=\\frac{RV_t}{F_t}-\\ln\\!\\frac{RV_t}{F_t}-1$$\n",
    "\n",
    "QLIKE is asymmetric: it penalises **under-prediction** of volatility more than over-prediction. This is why it is a useful complement to MSE, which treats both directions equally. Both losses are \"lower is better\".\n",
    "\n",
    "**Loss ratio.** I report $\\text{MSE}_{XGB}/\\text{MSE}_{EN}$ and $\\text{QLIKE}_{XGB}/\\text{QLIKE}_{EN}$. A ratio **below 1 means XGBoost has the lower average loss**, i.e. non-linearity helped. A ratio above 1 means EN is better.\n",
    "\n",
    "> `fc_level` is taken as given from the files. For both models it equals $\\exp(\\hat y_t+\\sigma^2_t/2)$, the log-normal retransformation using the `sigma2` column. I checked this on the data. If your group intended a different level forecast (e.g. $\\exp(\\hat y_t)$), QLIKE results will change."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def qlike(rv, f, normalised=True):\n",
    "    r = rv / f\n",
    "    return r - np.log(r) - 1.0 if normalised else r + np.log(f)\n",
    "\n",
    "panel[\"se_en\"]  = (panel.actual_logRV - panel.fc_log_en) ** 2\n",
    "panel[\"se_xgb\"] = (panel.actual_logRV - panel.fc_log_xgb) ** 2\n",
    "panel[\"ql_en\"]  = qlike(panel.actual_RV, panel.fc_level_en,  QLIKE_NORMALISED)\n",
    "panel[\"ql_xgb\"] = qlike(panel.actual_RV, panel.fc_level_xgb, QLIKE_NORMALISED)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Loss differential d_t = L_EN - L_XGB.   d_t > 0  <=>  XGB had the LOWER loss in that month.\n",
    "panel[\"d_mse\"]   = panel.se_en - panel.se_xgb\n",
    "panel[\"d_qlike\"] = panel.ql_en - panel.ql_xgb\n",
    "\n",
    "panel[[\"info_set\", \"etf\", \"date\", \"se_en\", \"se_xgb\", \"ql_en\", \"ql_xgb\", \"d_mse\", \"d_qlike\"]].head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Average losses and loss ratios (descriptive, before any test)\n",
    "\n",
    "Average loss over the out-of-sample window for each ETF and each cell. These are **point estimates**. Section 7 shows whether the differences are statistically distinguishable from zero."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def in_window(df, start, end):\n",
    "    out = df\n",
    "    if start is not None: out = out[out.date >= pd.Timestamp(start)]\n",
    "    if end   is not None: out = out[out.date <= pd.Timestamp(end)]\n",
    "    return out\n",
    "\n",
    "label_of = INFO_SETS\n",
    "loss_rows = []\n",
    "for sname, (s, e) in SUBSAMPLES.items():\n",
    "    sub = in_window(panel, s, e)\n",
    "    for key, g in sub.groupby(\"info_set\"):\n",
    "        for etf, gg in list(g.groupby(\"etf\")) + ([(\"ALL (avg)\", g)] if POOL_ETFS else []):\n",
    "            for model, se, ql in [(\"EN\", gg.se_en, gg.ql_en), (\"XGB\", gg.se_xgb, gg.ql_xgb)]:\n",
    "                loss_rows.append(dict(subsample=sname, info_set=key, etf=etf, model=model,\n",
    "                                      n=len(gg), mse_logrv=se.mean(), qlike=ql.mean()))\n",
    "losses = pd.DataFrame(loss_rows)\n",
    "\n",
    "ratio = (losses.pivot_table(index=[\"subsample\", \"info_set\", \"etf\"], columns=\"model\", values=[\"mse_logrv\", \"qlike\"])\n",
    "               .pipe(lambda x: pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "                   \"n\": losses.groupby([\"subsample\", \"info_set\", \"etf\"]).n.first(),\n",
    "                   \"MSE_EN\": x[(\"mse_logrv\", \"EN\")], \"MSE_XGB\": x[(\"mse_logrv\", \"XGB\")],\n",
    "                   \"MSE ratio XGB/EN\": x[(\"mse_logrv\", \"XGB\")] / x[(\"mse_logrv\", \"EN\")],\n",
    "                   \"QLIKE_EN\": x[(\"qlike\", \"EN\")], \"QLIKE_XGB\": x[(\"qlike\", \"XGB\")],\n",
    "                   \"QLIKE ratio XGB/EN\": x[(\"qlike\", \"XGB\")] / x[(\"qlike\", \"EN\")]})))\n",
    "ratio_display = ratio.reset_index()\n",
    "ratio_display[\"info_set\"] = ratio_display.info_set.map(label_of)\n",
    "ratio_display"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. The Diebold–Mariano test: what it does and why it is valid here\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea.** For each month compute the loss differential $d_t=L^{EN}_t-L^{XGB}_t$. Under the null of equal predictive accuracy, $E[d_t]=0$. The DM statistic is the standardised mean of $d_t$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$DM=\\frac{\\bar d}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(\\bar d)}},\\qquad \\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(\\bar d)=\\frac{1}{T}\\Big(\\hat\\gamma_0+2\\sum_{k=1}^{h-1}\\hat\\gamma_k\\Big)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\hat\\gamma_k$ is the sample autocovariance of $d_t$ at lag $k$.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Horizon.** With $h=1$ (one month ahead) the sum is empty. One-step-ahead errors are not expected to be serially correlated by construction, so no HAC lags are added. For $h>1$ the code includes the $h-1$ autocovariances (rectangular kernel).\n",
    "* **Small-sample correction (`HLN_CORRECTION=True`).** The statistic is multiplied by $\\sqrt{\\frac{T+1-2h+h(h-1)/T}{T}}$ (Harvey, Leybourne & Newbold, 1997) and compared with a Student-$t_{T-1}$ distribution instead of the normal. This is the standard fix when $T$ is only a couple of hundred months.\n",
    "* **Sign convention.** $d_t>0$ means XGB had the lower loss. I report:\n",
    "  * a **two-sided** p-value ($H_1$: the models differ), and\n",
    "  * a **one-sided** p-value ($H_1$: *XGB is better than EN*). This is the direct answer to *\"does non-linearity add anything?\"*.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why DM and not Clark–West here.** The professor's guidance: DM's non-standard distribution problem arises when comparing **nested** models, where the larger model collapses to the smaller one under the null. That happens for GARCH vs GARCH-MIDAS and for the EN information-set comparisons (no macro, no VIX, VIX only, full). EN and XGB are **different model classes** on the *same inputs*, so neither is a special case of the other. The pair is non-nested and DM with standard critical values is appropriate.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Per-ETF vs pooled.** Each ETF is tested separately ($T$ = number of out-of-sample months). If `POOL_ETFS=True`, I also test the **cross-ETF mean** of $d_t$ for each month. Averaging within a month first keeps the test statistic valid despite the strong correlation between ETF losses in the same month."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def dm_test(d, h=1, hln=True):\n",
    "    # Diebold-Mariano test on a loss-differential series d_t = L_A,t - L_B,t.\n",
    "    # Returns mean(d), DM statistic, two-sided p, and one-sided p for H1: mean(d) > 0 (B better than A).\n",
    "    d = np.asarray(d, dtype=float)\n",
    "    d = d[~np.isnan(d)]\n",
    "    T = len(d)\n",
    "    dbar = d.mean()\n",
    "    dc = d - dbar\n",
    "    var = (dc @ dc) / T                                  # gamma_0\n",
    "    for k in range(1, h):                                # + 2 * sum_{k=1}^{h-1} gamma_k\n",
    "        var += 2.0 * (dc[k:] @ dc[:-k]) / T\n",
    "    if var <= 0:\n",
    "        return dict(T=T, mean_d=dbar, dm_stat=np.nan, p_two_sided=np.nan, p_xgb_better=np.nan)\n",
    "    stat = dbar / np.sqrt(var / T)\n",
    "    if hln:\n",
    "        stat *= np.sqrt((T + 1 - 2 * h + h * (h - 1) / T) / T)\n",
    "        p2, p1 = 2 * stats.t.sf(abs(stat), T - 1), stats.t.sf(stat, T - 1)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        p2, p1 = 2 * stats.norm.sf(abs(stat)), stats.norm.sf(stat)\n",
    "    return dict(T=T, mean_d=dbar, dm_stat=stat, p_two_sided=p2, p_xgb_better=p1)\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- sanity check on simulated data: DM should reject rarely when the null is true, often when it is false ---\n",
    "rng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n",
    "null_rej = np.mean([dm_test(rng.normal(0.0, 1, 214), H, HLN_CORRECTION)[\"p_two_sided\"] < ALPHA for _ in range(2000)])\n",
    "alt_rej  = np.mean([dm_test(rng.normal(0.3, 1, 214), H, HLN_CORRECTION)[\"p_two_sided\"] < ALPHA for _ in range(2000)])\n",
    "print(f\"Simulation check (T=214): rejection rate under H0 = {null_rej:.3f} (target {ALPHA}), under a true mean of 0.3 = {alt_rej:.3f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Run the DM tests\n",
    "\n",
    "For every **sub-sample × cell × ETF** (and the pooled average), I test both loss functions. The table also repeats the loss ratio, so the size and significance of each difference sit side by side."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def pooled_series(g, col):\n",
    "    # cross-ETF mean of the loss differential for each date (only dates where all ETFs are present)\n",
    "    wide = g.pivot(index=\"date\", columns=\"etf\", values=col)\n",
    "    return wide.dropna().mean(axis=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "dm_rows = []\n",
    "for sname, (s, e) in SUBSAMPLES.items():\n",
    "    sub = in_window(panel, s, e)\n",
    "    for key in INFO_SETS:\n",
    "        g = sub[sub.info_set == key]\n",
    "        targets = [(etf, gg.sort_values(\"date\")) for etf, gg in g.groupby(\"etf\")]\n",
    "        for etf, gg in targets:\n",
    "            for loss_name, dcol, a, b in [(\"MSE (log RV)\", \"d_mse\", \"se_en\", \"se_xgb\"),\n",
    "                                          (\"QLIKE (RV level)\", \"d_qlike\", \"ql_en\", \"ql_xgb\")]:\n",
    "                res = dm_test(gg[dcol], H, HLN_CORRECTION)\n",
    "                dm_rows.append(dict(subsample=sname, info_set=key, etf=etf, loss=loss_name,\n",
    "                                    loss_EN=gg[a].mean(), loss_XGB=gg[b].mean(), **res))\n",
    "        if POOL_ETFS:\n",
    "            for loss_name, dcol, a, b in [(\"MSE (log RV)\", \"d_mse\", \"se_en\", \"se_xgb\"),\n",
    "                                          (\"QLIKE (RV level)\", \"d_qlike\", \"ql_en\", \"ql_xgb\")]:\n",
    "                d_pool = pooled_series(g, dcol)\n",
    "                res = dm_test(d_pool, H, HLN_CORRECTION)\n",
    "                dm_rows.append(dict(subsample=sname, info_set=key, etf=\"ALL (avg)\", loss=loss_name,\n",
    "                                    loss_EN=g[a].mean(), loss_XGB=g[b].mean(), **res))\n",
    "\n",
    "dm = pd.DataFrame(dm_rows)\n",
    "dm[\"ratio_XGB_over_EN\"] = dm.loss_XGB / dm.loss_EN\n",
    "dm[\"sig_two_sided_5pct\"] = dm.p_two_sided < ALPHA\n",
    "dm[\"winner\"] = np.where(dm.p_two_sided < ALPHA, np.where(dm.mean_d > 0, \"XGB\", \"EN\"), \"no sig. difference\")\n",
    "dm[\"cell\"] = dm.info_set.map(INFO_SETS)\n",
    "\n",
    "show = dm[[\"subsample\", \"cell\", \"etf\", \"loss\", \"T\", \"loss_EN\", \"loss_XGB\", \"ratio_XGB_over_EN\",\n",
    "           \"dm_stat\", \"p_two_sided\", \"p_xgb_better\", \"winner\"]]\n",
    "display(show)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. Summary and visuals\n",
    "\n",
    "### 8a. One table per loss: pooled result per cell\n",
    "The headline answer to *\"does non-linearity add anything?\"*, one row per cell."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "if POOL_ETFS:\n",
    "    for loss_name in [\"MSE (log RV)\", \"QLIKE (RV level)\"]:\n",
    "        t = dm[(dm.etf == \"ALL (avg)\") & (dm.loss == loss_name)][\n",
    "            [\"subsample\", \"cell\", \"loss_EN\", \"loss_XGB\", \"ratio_XGB_over_EN\", \"dm_stat\", \"p_two_sided\", \"p_xgb_better\", \"winner\"]]\n",
    "        print(f\"\\n=== {loss_name} · cross-ETF average ===\")\n",
    "        display(t.reset_index(drop=True))\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print(\"POOL_ETFS=False: see the per-ETF table above.\")"
