    "## 10. How to describe this in the report\n",
    "\n",
    "**Method (can be used almost as written):**\n",
    "> EN and XGBoost are compared on identical information sets using MSE on log RV and QLIKE on RV levels. Because the two models belong to different model classes and neither nests the other, we assess differences in predictive accuracy with the Diebold–Mariano test (one-step-ahead horizon, Harvey–Leybourne–Newbold small-sample correction). The Clark–West test, which is designed for nested comparisons, is used for the EN information-set comparisons instead.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Things to be upfront about:**\n",
    "* The test is run **per ETF** and on the cross-ETF average. Several tests are run, so isolated 5% rejections should be read cautiously.\n",
    "* An **insignificant** DM result means we cannot distinguish the models. It does not show they are equally good.\n",
    "* Results can be driven by a few extreme months. Check the cumulative-differential plot (8c) before drawing conclusions from the full-sample average.\n",
    "* The XGB file has one extra forecast month (January 2008) that EN does not. It is dropped so both models are scored on the same months (section 3)."
   ]
